Original commit body from 92d74c9bea99b116041578cdcedfbd3a28ae3c35:

Rust emitted the `enum` and `trait` CONTAINERS but nothing inside them,
so the containment linker had nothing to root and a reverse slice from
either returned the container alone -- which a consumer reads as "this
enum is dead" / "this trait has no users". Drains both of rust's
container-member parity holes.

Three distinct gaps behind one symptom, each measured:

  * an `enum_variant` produced no symbol at all;
  * a `function_signature_item` (trait method, no default body) produced
    no symbol at all;
  * a `function_item` INSIDE a `trait_item` (a method WITH a default
    body) DID produce one -- but as kind="function" with a BARE name
    (`area`, not `Drawable::area`), indistinguishable from a
    module-level free function and unrooted by the containment linker,
    whose parent extraction needs a separator in the name.

The third is the one a naive fix misses: it was already emitting a
symbol inside the trait's span, so a test asserting only "the container
has a member" would have passed against the bug.

Naming follows what the file already does -- `rust.py` builds
`{impl_target}::{func_name}` for impl methods and already keys enum
variant field types as `{enum_name}::{variant_name}`. `::` is one of the
containment linker's separators, so a member named `Owner::member` roots
for free. Variants are kind="field" (the shape rust.py's own struct-field
loop uses, and the choice D and Nim already made); trait members are
kind="method".

Measured end-to-end:

  slice --entry Color    --language rust --reverse   1 node  -> 4
  slice --entry Drawable --language rust --reverse   2 nodes -> 4

The old 2 for Drawable came via `implements` edges, not class expansion,
so a node-count check on a trait would have read as a false pass.

The trait owner is a SEPARATE lookup from the impl target, deliberately.
`_get_impl_target` has six callers, most in call/receiver resolution
where it answers "what type is `self` here?" -- and a trait is not a type
a receiver can be, so widening it would silently change how those sites
resolve. `_get_trait_owner` is used only by the emission paths and stops
at the first `impl_item`, because in `impl Drawable for Service` the
methods belong to Service. Pinned by a regression test (`Service::draw`
present, `Drawable::run` absent) plus a direct white-box test of the
impl-stops-the-walk branch, which the production caller short-circuits
(`owner = impl_target or _get_trait_owner(...)`) and which is
load-bearing rather than decorative.

An existing gate caught a real cross-module break, which is why this
touches two source files. `rust_scip.compute_rust_stable_id_from_source`
recomputes rust.py's identities from source so the SCIP-backed
rust-analyzer pass and the tree-sitter pass can dedup (WI-zakub parity).
It walked `function_item` only, so the moment rust.py emitted a
trait-signature method the parity test failed with `helper=None`.
rust-analyzer's SCIP emit DOES index trait method declarations, so these
are exactly the symbols both passes see. Node-type filter and owner
resolution are now mirrored there, and the new branch computes identity
exactly as the `function_item` branch does (normalized signature in the
type slot, stable_id=None when normalization fails).

`_extract_rust_signature` also accepts `function_signature_item`: that
node carries the same parameters/return_type fields and differs only in
having no body, which the extractor never read. For a trait contract the
signature is the member's entire content, so declining to extract it
would make the emitted symbol strictly less useful than its impl-side
counterpart.

Ratchet promotion was forced, not remembered: the G2 holes are
xfail(strict=True), so both rust cells XPASSed and FAILED the run until
removed from KNOWN_HOLES in this same commit. Remaining: 4 of 5
analyzers still emit no enum variants (typescript, java, csharp, swift);
2 of 6 still miss abstract-type members (typescript, swift). The spec's
scope note is updated to match -- it named rust as vacuous as of the
previous commit.

